This is more a Linux Module design issue than a driver bug.

=46or instance, dpt_i2o.c has the following fragment:

static struct pci_device_id dptids[] =3D {
         { PCI_DPT_VENDOR_ID, PCI_DPT_DEVICE_ID, PCI_ANY_ID, =20
PCI_ANY_ID,},
         { PCI_DPT_VENDOR_ID, PCI_DPT_RAPTOR_DEVICE_ID, PCI_ANY_ID, =20
PCI_ANY_ID,},
         { 0, }
};
MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE(pci,dptids);

which kind of depends on MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE doing its job. But you =20
are compiling the driver into the kernel rather than as a module and =20
the net result is the warning message.

MODULE_DEVICE_TABLE, in drivers compiled as build-in to the kernel =20
needs to 'use' it's arguments in some manner that
a) suppresses the warning
b) does not take up any space

Or one could add an #ifndef CONFIG_SCSI_DPT_I2O wrapped around the =20
above to suppress the warning...<ick>

Sincerely -- Mark Salyzyn

